{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014PiEunYYGmwSVQ4jJNEfBT","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→id: prd028-uniq\n     2→title: \"cmd/uniq: Report or Filter Adjacent Duplicate Lines\"\n     3→\n     4→problem: |\n     5→  macOS BSD uniq lacks some GNU uniq flags (-D, --group) and has minor output differences\n     6→  in edge cases. Scripts that deduplicate sorted data or count occurrences of lines depend\n     7→  on GNU uniq behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary\n     8→  (guniq, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by\n     9→  differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n    10→\n    11→goals:\n    12→  - G1: Remove or report adjacent duplicate lines from input, matching GNU uniq behavior.\n    13→  - G2: Support output filter flags (-d, -D, -u) to select only duplicate or only unique lines.\n    14→  - G3: Support -c to prefix each output line with a count of occurrences.\n    15→  - G4: Support field-skipping and character-skipping flags (-f, -s, -w) and case-insensitive comparison (-i).\n    16→\n    17→requirements:\n    18→  R1:\n    19→    title: Default deduplication\n    20→    items:\n    21→      - R1.1: By default, must read each line and write it once, suppressing all but the first occurrence of any run of identical adjacent lines.\n    22→      - R1.2: Lines that appear only once must be written. Lines that appear multiple times in a run must be written once. Non-adjacent duplicates are unaffected (uniq does not sort input).\n    23→      - R1.3: Must read from stdin when no file argument is given. Must accept an optional output file as a second argument.\n    24→      - R1.4: Comparison is case-sensitive by default. Each line includes the newline terminator in the comparison.\n    25→\n    26→  R2:\n    27→    title: Output selection (-d, -D, -u, -c)\n    28→    items:\n    29→      - R2.1: -d must print only lines that appear more than once in a run (one copy per run). Lines that appear only once are suppressed.\n    30→      - R2.2: -D must print all lines of each run that appears more than once. Every copy of a duplicated run is written, not just the first.\n    31→      - R2.3: -u must print only lines that appear exactly once (runs of one).\n    32→      - R2.4: -c must prefix each output line with the count of occurrences in its run, right-justified in a field wide enough to hold the count, followed by a space and the line.\n    33→\n    34→  R3:\n    35→    title: Comparison options (-i, -f, -s, -w)\n    36→    items:\n    37→      - R3.1: -i must perform case-insensitive comparison. Lines that differ only in case are treated as duplicates.\n    38→      - R3.2: -f N must skip the first N fields (whitespace-separated tokens) of each line before comparing. The skipped fields are still written to output.\n    39→      - R3.3: -s N must skip the first N characters of each line before comparing. The skipped characters are still written to output.\n    40→      - R3.4: -w N must compare only the first N characters of each line (after -f and -s adjustments). Characters beyond N are ignored for comparison purposes but still written.\n    41→\n    42→  R4:\n    43→    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    44→    items:\n    45→      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n    46→      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr.\n    47→      - R4.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n    48→      - R4.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n    49→\n    50→non_goals:\n    51→  - cmd/uniq does not sort its input; it operates only on adjacent duplicates.\n    52→  - cmd/uniq does not implement the --group flag available in some GNU versions; the core flags above are sufficient.\n    53→\n    54→acceptance_criteria:\n    55→  - \"AC1: printf 'a\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\na\\\\n' | uniq produces 'a\\\\nb\\\\na\\\\n', matching guniq.\"\n    56→  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\n' | uniq -c produces '      2 a\\\\n      1 b\\\\n', matching guniq -c.\"\n    57→  - \"AC3: printf 'a\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\n' | uniq -d produces 'a\\\\n', matching guniq -d.\"\n    58→  - \"AC4: printf 'a\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\n' | uniq -u produces 'b\\\\n', matching guniq -u.\"\n    59→  - \"AC5: printf 'A\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\n' | uniq -i produces 'A\\\\nb\\\\n', matching guniq -i.\"\n    60→  - \"AC6: Differential test against guniq passes for all required test cases.\"\n    61→  - \"AC7: cmd/uniq compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n    62→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80e98de1-9ba5-41b6-9248-92412e595065","uuid":"2def65db-53ae-431a-9908-c9872d3c4f31","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/357/docs/specs/product-requirements/prd028-uniq.yaml","content":"id: prd028-uniq\ntitle: \"cmd/uniq: Report or Filter Adjacent Duplicate Lines\"\n\nproblem: |\n  macOS BSD uniq lacks some GNU uniq flags (-D, --group) and has minor output differences\n  in edge cases. Scripts that deduplicate sorted data or count occurrences of lines depend\n  on GNU uniq behavior. The Go implementation must match the Homebrew GNU reference binary\n  (guniq, installed by coreutils) byte-for-byte on all flag combinations, verified by\n  differential testing via pkg/testutils.\n\ngoals:\n  - G1: Remove or report adjacent duplicate lines from input, matching GNU uniq behavior.\n  - G2: Support output filter flags (-d, -D, -u) to select only duplicate or only unique lines.\n  - G3: Support -c to prefix each output line with a count of occurrences.\n  - G4: Support field-skipping and character-skipping flags (-f, -s, -w) and case-insensitive comparison (-i).\n\nrequirements:\n  R1:\n    title: Default deduplication\n    items:\n      - R1.1: By default, must read each line and write it once, suppressing all but the first occurrence of any run of identical adjacent lines.\n      - R1.2: Lines that appear only once must be written. Lines that appear multiple times in a run must be written once. Non-adjacent duplicates are unaffected (uniq does not sort input).\n      - R1.3: Must read from stdin when no file argument is given. Must accept an optional output file as a second argument.\n      - R1.4: Comparison is case-sensitive by default. Each line includes the newline terminator in the comparison.\n\n  R2:\n    title: Output selection (-d, -D, -u, -c)\n    items:\n      - R2.1: -d must print only lines that appear more than once in a run (one copy per run). Lines that appear only once are suppressed.\n      - R2.2: -D must print all lines of each run that appears more than once. Every copy of a duplicated run is written, not just the first.\n      - R2.3: -u must print only lines that appear exactly once (runs of one).\n      - R2.4: -c must prefix each output line with the count of occurrences in its run, right-justified in a field wide enough to hold the count, followed by a space and the line.\n\n  R3:\n    title: Comparison options (-i, -f, -s, -w)\n    items:\n      - R3.1: -i must perform case-insensitive comparison. Lines that differ only in case are treated as duplicates.\n      - R3.2: -f N must skip the first N fields (whitespace-separated tokens) of each line before comparing. The skipped fields are still written to output.\n      - R3.3: -s N must skip the first N characters of each line before comparing. The skipped characters are still written to output.\n      - R3.4: -w N must compare only the first N characters of each line (after -f and -s adjustments). Characters beyond N are ignored for comparison purposes but still written.\n\n  R4:\n    title: Exit codes and SIGPIPE\n    items:\n      - R4.1: Must exit 0 when all inputs are processed successfully.\n      - R4.2: Must exit 1 when any input file cannot be opened, printing the error to stderr.\n      - R4.3: Must exit 1 when a write error occurs on stdout.\n      - R4.4: Must handle SIGPIPE gracefully (exit 0) using pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler.\n\nnon_goals:\n  - cmd/uniq does not sort its input; it operates only on adjacent duplicates.\n  - cmd/uniq does not implement the --group flag available in some GNU versions; the core flags above are sufficient.\n\nacceptance_criteria:\n  - \"AC1: printf 'a\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\na\\\\n' | uniq produces 'a\\\\nb\\\\na\\\\n', matching guniq.\"\n  - \"AC2: printf 'a\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\n' | uniq -c produces '      2 a\\\\n      1 b\\\\n', matching guniq -c.\"\n  - \"AC3: printf 'a\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\n' | uniq -d produces 'a\\\\n', matching guniq -d.\"\n  - \"AC4: printf 'a\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\n' | uniq -u produces 'b\\\\n', matching guniq -u.\"\n  - \"AC5: printf 'A\\\\na\\\\nb\\\\n' | uniq -i produces 'A\\\\nb\\\\n', matching guniq -i.\"\n  - \"AC6: Differential test against guniq passes for all required test cases.\"\n  - \"AC7: cmd/uniq compiles with no warnings under golangci-lint.\"\n","numLines":62,"startLine":1,"totalLines":62}}}
